uld be the winning tile? No, T8 is in hand but not used. That means we must discard T8 before winning. So we can't have T8 in final hand. So we must discard T8 at some point. So discard W9 now, keep T3,T5,T5,T8, then discard T8 later if we draw T4. But if we draw T6, we can form T5-T6-T7 (need T6,T7) but we only have T5,T8. T8 doesn't help. So only T4 draw helps.  \n\nBetter: discard T8 now, keep T3,T5,T5,W9. Then draw T4 \u2192 T3-T4-T5 meld, T5 pair, W9 needs T7+T8 or T7+T7? W9 needs T7,T8 to form meld, but T8 is gone. So W9 is dead unless we draw W7,W8. Unlikely.  \n\nActually, simplest route: keep T5 pair, hope to complete a chow with T3 and T8/W9. T3 needs T1,T2,T4. T8 needs T6,T7,T9. W9 needs T7,T8. Overlap: T7 helps W9+T8. So if we keep T8 and W9, drawing T7 gives T7-T8-T9 chow. Then T3 can be paired with drawn T2 or T1 or T4 for another chow, but we already have 4 melds then (3 exposed + T7T8T9), and T5 pair. That works. So keep T8 and W9, discard T3.  \n\nDiscard T3 now: hand T5,T5,T8,W9. Draw T7 (2 left) \u2192 chow T7-T8-T9, T5 pair, done. Also draw T5 (3 left) \u2192 T5 pung, pair W9 or T8. Also draw T8 (3 left) \u2192 T8 pair, need meld from T5,W9 + ? T5+W9 need T4 or T6? Not possible. So T7 draw is best.  \n\nT7 availability: 2 left, decent.  \n\nOther discards:  \n- Discard W9: need T4 for T3-T4-T5 meld, then T8 alone \u2192 must discard T8 later, but then need another draw to replace T8 with a pair? Actually after T3-T4-T5 meld, we have T5 pair, T8 alone. To win, we must draw T8 for pair, then winning tile is T8. Possible. T8: 3 left.  \n- Discard T8: need T4 for T3-T4-T5 meld, then W9 alone \u2192 must pair W9, need draw W9 (1 left) or use W9 in chow (needs W7,W8).  \n- Discard T5: breaks pair, harder.  \n\nSo between discard T3 and discard W9:  \nDiscard T3: wait for T7 or T5 draw.  \nDiscard W9: wait for T4 draw, then T8 draw.  \n\nProbability: T7: 2, T5: 3 total 5 outs for T3 discard.  \nT4: 3, then T8: 3, but need two draws in sequence, less likely.  \n\nSo discard T3 is better.  \n\n**Reason:**  \nDiscarding T3 keeps T5 pair and the W9-T8 combination that can form a chow with a T7 draw (2 left), or a T5 draw gives a pung. This offers the most immediate winning chances with fewer tiles needed.  \n\n**Answer:** Play T3"}
